# F-Droid index metadata for com.hrbons.wordpuzzle.
# Sourced by ../rebuild-index.sh — plain shell assignments, no jq needed.
# NAME matches the launcher label (values/strings.xml app_name), not the package name:
# the package is historically "wordpuzzle" while the app ships as Word Cross (values-nl
# says "Woord Puzzel", but the index carries one name and the repo is English).
# The puzzles and the interface are Dutch — the SUMMARY says so, so nobody installs it
# expecting English clues.
#
# LICENSE is a deliberate choice here, not inherited (2026-07-27, user): the source repo
# abons/wordpuzzle-src is private and carries no LICENSE file, so "Proprietary" is the only
# factually true value. MIT would grant rights over code nobody can obtain, and the apps are
# meant to earn — that is not a right to give away. Going Proprietary -> MIT later is free;
# the reverse retracts a granted licence. The open question is not this field but whether the
# source goes public at all, and that is one decision across wordpuzzle/wordfinder/fastlane.
# (wordguesser is MIT because abons/wordguesser-src really is public, with a LICENSE file.)
NAME="Word Cross"
SUMMARY="Dutch crossword puzzles from word definitions - tiny, native, offline"
DESCRIPTION="A tiny, native Android crossword game: an interlocking Dutch crossword in which every clue is the answer's own dictionary definition, with the answer itself masked out. Free play offers three puzzle sizes, and a seeded daily puzzle gives everyone the same grid on the same day, alongside statistics and a daily streak. Every puzzle sits on a procedurally drawn scene derived from its seed, so the game ships no image assets at all. The grid is published to screen readers as a virtual view tree, so it can be played with TalkBack. Dutch word lists with offline definitions (Dutch Wiktionary, CC BY-SA 3.0). Pure Kotlin on the Android framework only: no ads, no tracking, no third-party libraries. The puzzles and the interface are in Dutch."
LICENSE="Proprietary"
CATEGORIES='["Games"]'
